@h1 Tables h1@

A Wit table is one node. Hand it rows and it draws a full
@@@code <table> code@@@ — caption, header, body — with no grid of pipes to align.
Rows can come as inline text, as a collection of records, or as @code @row code@ /
@code @col code@ child nodes when cells need links and emphasis.

@h2 A quick table from inline rows h2@

Pass a list of rows in the @code rows code@ parameter. Row 0 is the header; every
later row is a body row. A @code caption code@ rides above the table.

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Format, Extension], [Web page, .html], [Markdown, .md], [Print, .pdf]]| |caption Output formats|
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Output formats</caption><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Extension</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Web page</td><td>.html</td></tr><tr><td>Markdown</td><td>.md</td></tr><tr><td>Print</td><td>.pdf</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

Drop the header with @@@code |header false| code@@@ — the first row becomes a
plain body row:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Web page, .html], [Markdown, .md]]| |header false|
→ <table><tbody><tr><td>Web page</td><td>.html</td></tr><tr><td>Markdown</td><td>.md</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@aside(class note) 💡 Inline cells split on commas, and a comma inside a cell
can't be escaped — a stray comma makes an extra cell. Keep inline cell text
comma-free, or use the body form below where each cell is its own node. aside@

@h2 A table from a data collection h2@

Point @code rows code@ at a collection of records and each record becomes a row.
With no schema, the columns come from the first record's keys:

@@@pre
#packages: [ { name - parser, role - reads source }, { name - runtime, role - resolves data } ]

@table |rows @packages|
pre@@@

Emits headings @code name code@ / @code role code@ over the two records. To choose
and label the columns yourself, add a schema — @@@code |schema [name, role]| code@@@
for raw keys in order, or @@@code |schema { name - Package, role - Role }| code@@@
for display headings. Loading the records from a file at build time works the same
way; see @a |href /docs/guides/data/| Data a@.

@h2 Rich cells: the body form h2@

The inline and collection forms hold plain strings. When a cell needs a link,
emphasis, or a nested node, author the rows as child nodes instead: a
@@@code @row code@@@ per row, a @@@code @col code@@@ per cell. Leave off the
@code rows code@ parameter and the renderer reads the body.

@@@pre
@table |caption Modules|
  @row @col Module col@ @col Notes col@ row@
  @row @col Parser col@ @col Reads *source*; see @a |href /docs/reference/syntax/| the syntax a@. col@ row@
table@
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Modules</caption><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parser</td><td>Reads <strong>source</strong>; see <a href="/docs/reference/syntax/">the syntax</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

The first @@@code @row code@@@ is the header — its cells render as
@@@code <th> code@@@ — unless you pass @code header false code@. A
@@@code @th code@@@ cell is always a header cell, even inside a body row. The
HTML-style names @@@code @tr code@@@ / @@@code @td code@@@ / @@@code @th code@@@
work as aliases for @@@code @row code@@@ / @@@code @col code@@@, and the whole row
can sit on one line: @@@code @row @col a col@ @col b col@ row@ code@@@.

@@@code @row code@@@ and @@@code @col code@@@ are interchangeable: the outer node
is the row, the inner one the cell, whichever keyword you use. So
@@@code @col @row a row@ @row b row@ col@ code@@@ builds the same row as
@@@code @row @col a col@ @col b col@ row@ code@@@.

@h2 Data-driven rows with rich cells h2@

This is the form the others can't reach: rows generated from data, with cells that
still hold nodes. Wrap the @@@code @row code@@@ line in
@@@code (each … as …) code@@@ and pull fields into each cell body.

@@@pre
#modules: [ { name - Parser, status - stable }, { name - Runtime, status - stable } ]

@table |caption Modules|
  @row @col Module col@ @col Status col@ row@
  (each @modules as m)
  @row @col @m.name col@ @col @em @m.status em@ col@ row@
  (end)
table@
pre@@@

Emits:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Modules</caption><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parser</td><td><em>stable</em></td></tr><tr><td>Runtime</td><td><em>stable</em></td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

The header row stays fixed; the loop appends one body row per record. Put an
@@@code (if …) code@@@ inside the loop to filter which rows appear — here only the
stable modules:

@@@pre
#modules: [ { name - Parser, status - stable }, { name - Sandbox, status - draft } ]

@table |caption Stable modules|
  @row @col Module col@ @col Status col@ row@
  (each @modules as m)
  (if @m.status is stable)
  @row @col @m.name col@ @col @em @m.status em@ col@ row@
  (end)
  (end)
table@
pre@@@

The draft row drops out; only Parser survives:

@@@pre
<table><caption>Stable modules</caption><thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parser</td><td><em>stable</em></td></tr></tbody></table>
pre@@@

@h2 One limitation: data resolves in the body, not in a parameter h2@

A field reference resolves inside a cell @strong body strong@ — @@@code @col @m.name col@ code@@@
works — but @strong not inside a parameter strong@. A link's @code href code@
cannot yet be @@@code @m.url code@@@:

@@@pre
@col @a |href @m.url| @m.name a@ col@   ✗ the href is not resolved
@col @a |href /modules/parser| @m.name a@ col@   ✓ literal href, data in the text
pre@@@

Keep every data-driven value in the cell body and write parameters as literals.

